    {
      "path": "/mnt/nas/data/gaojing/InorganicSys/2018/downloads/pdfs/10/3938/jkps/74/187/_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md",
      "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr",
      "line": 26,
      "text": "Figure 1 shows the TG curve of the LLZO precursor solution fired in air in the temperature range from 30 to 1000 ◦C. The first weight change was observed for the disappearance of the residual solvent in the range 100 - 120 ◦C. The decomposition of PVP, which was used as a chelating agent, occurred at 330 ◦C, and the formation of the initial garnet phase through decomposition of the nitrate species and organic materials occurred at 350 - 600 ◦C. No significant weight change was observed at temperatures above 600 - 700 ◦C, indicating that the transformation of the mixture precursor to a crystalline oxide starts at temperatures above this range. Based on this observation, the cubic phase was confirmed to have been formed most stably at a temperature of 800 ◦C or higher.",
